          "content": [
            "# Virtual Memory in 5 Bullet Points\n\n- **What it is:** Virtual memory gives each process its own large, private address space (e.g., \"your program can use 4GB\"), even if the physical RAM is smaller. The OS and hardware translate virtual addresses to real physical addresses behind the scenes.\n\n- **Paging:** Memory is divided into fixed-size chunks called *pages* (typically 4KB). Virtual pages are mapped to physical frames via a page table. This lets the OS place pages anywhere in RAM and avoids fragmenting memory.\n\n- **Page faults:** When your program accesses a page not currently in RAM, the CPU raises a *page fault*. The OS pauses the program, loads the page from disk (swap file), updates the page table, and resumes execution. This is how the OS can \"extend\" RAM using disk — but disk access is ~100,000x slower, so frequent faults kill performance.\n\n- **TLB (Translation Lookaside Buffer):** A small, ultra-fast cache in the CPU that stores recent virtual→physical address translations. Without it, every memory access would need an extra page table lookup. With it, most translations resolve in ~1 cycle. A TLB miss means walking the page table (or a page fault if the page isn't in RAM).\n\n- **Why you care:** It enables process isolation (one buggy program can't corrupt another's memory), lets you run programs bigger than RAM, and explains why *locality of reference* (accessing nearby data repeatedly) matters — good locality keeps pages in RAM and the TLB warm, avoiding expensive faults."
          ],
